#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167558} :activity/id #uuid "a3dd6621-c82e-571c-bf04-7adea5a2cf8e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049899 :topic/id #uuid "1cb109e6-3987-5c41-9a55-81f03d70697e" :topic/math-academy-id 3274} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168157 :tutorial/content "When performing certain types of statistical tests (e.g., hypothesis testing), we often need to use standard cumulative distribution function (CDF) tables to carry out the test.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to construct CDF tables for the Poisson distribution and use them to calculate probabilities.\n\nFirst, recall that the probability mass function of the Poisson distribution $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ is a positive parameter.\n\nThe CDF of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's compute the values of $F(x)$ for the distribution $\\textrm{Po}(2)$ at $x=0$ and $x=1.$  \n\nTo do that, we use the definition of the cumulative distribution function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =P(X\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(X=0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{0}\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{0!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{1} \\\\  & ≈0.1353 \\\\ F(1) & =P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{0}\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{0!}+\\frac{{2}^{1}\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{1!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{1}+\\frac{2\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{1} \\\\  & ≈0.4060\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe CDF data is usually presented as a table. If we were to continue this process and calculate $F(2), F(3), F(4),$ and $F(5),$ our table would look as follows:\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,0.1353\\,$ | $\\,0.4060\\,$ | $\\,0.6767\\,$ | $\\,0.8571\\,$ | $\\,0.9473\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\cdots\\,\\,\\,$ |\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $F(x)$ is an increasing function.\n\n- Since $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ takes arbitrarily large values, we have $F(x) < 1$ for all $n,$ and $F(x) \\to 1$ as $x\\to\\infty.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7f81a6db-33d2-5a6a-98e7-852037072330" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9443 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7a435309-f7d2-561b-9e2d-96419cf972f5" :step/math-academy-id 221725 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166498 :question/id #uuid "d8c1a664-c40a-5525-b1c0-2f977c01077a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15057"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387be734-719f-52cd-8079-50c744ecdd5e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049358}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488286}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Poisson CDF Table"} :step/id #uuid "33739b57-c32a-5436-8a2f-c7b0a78550cc" :step/math-academy-id 221726 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166499 :question/id #uuid "a4d766f8-8e20-52a0-afa1-0eea74e6688e" :question/math-academy-id "e-15058"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "240ed915-56cd-5e22-8c7f-a36ebea90279" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503716}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Poisson Probability Using a CDF Table"} :step/id #uuid "eb901a88-539f-5c1d-8161-d1cc6b32b4bb" :step/math-academy-id 221729 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166500 :question/id #uuid "b73faba8-1021-5d9c-94f5-5e1b908397b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-15059"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e597d4c-f86f-509e-9fc8-7dd7c03125a1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503735}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Poisson Probability Over an Interval Using a CDF Table"} :step/id #uuid "c2b9888b-da36-5ea0-907a-42747b8715c1" :step/math-academy-id 221732 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166501 :question/id #uuid "7fb51db3-deb3-5c76-985f-3e43b7cfe995" :question/math-academy-id "e-15060"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e822ac5d-67a0-57b5-b5fe-d3ad03cd91fe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503693}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Containing a \"Greater Than\" Symbol"} :step/id #uuid "ad4c4a48-5234-5c54-86de-eb13ccd09abb" :step/math-academy-id 221735}] :activity/title "The CDF of the Poisson Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167037}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503807} :activity/id #uuid "a0a97922-c29e-5eb9-a00b-a08031d2cade" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049899 :topic/id #uuid "1cb109e6-3987-5c41-9a55-81f03d70697e" :topic/math-academy-id 3274} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "380c1b05-6c1c-5ffd-bc5c-a04c05c5c687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503080} {:answer/id #uuid "5577f8d1-9f94-5c6f-ba9c-92f1736f818d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4558" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503144} {:answer/id #uuid "6e80960c-3886-58dd-b64a-4e2557138902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503203} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d579dc-32ed-5798-98c2-1ace09760f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3516" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503400} {:answer/id #uuid "d834d903-ffee-58a0-92db-9ab0051f1e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5470" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503412}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503412} :answer-field/id #uuid "a20cf601-9154-52ab-ba75-6f001245e333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b92762e3-576d-55cb-b009-a97e0be6338a" :question/math-academy-id "q-225067" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(4)$ given below, compute $P(3\\le X<6).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0916\\,$ | $\\,0.2381\\,$ | $\\,0.4335\\,$ | $\\,0.6288\\,$ | $\\,0.7851\\,$ | $\\,0.8893\\,$ | $\\,0.9489\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(5) & =P(X<6) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2\\})+P(X\\in \\{3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 2)+P(3\\le X<6) \\\\  & =F(2)+P(3\\le X<6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X<6) & =F(5)-F(2) \\\\  & =0.7851-0.2381 \\\\  & =0.5470.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "21d06828-9a3e-5396-9154-89624675cb97" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503827}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503803 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79a1b4b4-0c8f-509b-96be-e81e96d0cf38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503229} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bb07aaf-fd25-5aa9-a148-43a824ecca08" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3df054eb-2d42-57cd-ac48-5335659c80b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-225055" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(5)$ given below, compute $P(2\\le X\\le 4).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0067\\,$ | $\\,0.0404\\,$ | $\\,0.1247\\,$ | $\\,0.2650\\,$ | $\\,0.4405\\,$ | $\\,0.6160\\,$ | $\\,0.7622\\,$ |\n\n$P(2\\le X\\le 4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\})+P(X\\in \\{2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 1)+P(2\\le X\\le 4) \\\\  & =F(1)+P(2\\le X\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 4) & =F(4)-F(1) \\\\  & =0.4405-0.0404 \\\\  & =0.4001.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "84e1ee1a-4b89-5aa4-b30a-3a5d79ef978f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503771}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072af78c-0cf5-586f-bc53-ad8197623ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3239" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502995} {:answer/id #uuid "23093e91-9f8a-526f-b9fe-a4c366c326dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2068" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503048} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c5ad47-e307-560d-aa30-f635fcf6ddd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1171" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503318} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc0b37a-e911-5ce6-b543-981451964978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5307" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503430} {:answer/id #uuid "fe79ce86-a670-57ab-ab9f-302046099afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4557" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502995} :answer-field/id #uuid "03d92468-cb05-508e-bcd0-42d2b482d3c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "951aecd2-9a03-5921-a647-8badf487101e" :question/math-academy-id "q-224995" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(9)$ given below, find the value of $P(X\\le 7).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0212\\,$ | $\\,0.0550\\,$ | $\\,0.1157\\,$ | $\\,0.2068\\,$ | $\\,0.3239\\,$ | $\\,0.4557\\,$ | $\\,0.5874\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7) & =F(7) \\\\  & =0.3239.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8fc62da4-3a4c-5a53-bfc2-793fee1d81cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "871b12f6-b300-5aad-9c37-0f6b3ba75025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7664" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503257} {:answer/id #uuid "b364a7c4-21e7-540e-8e24-cda36eac8906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503347} {:answer/id #uuid "bf85c091-12aa-55ce-9c65-94896b0eada1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7166" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503363} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a0a61b-0e09-5891-9fdf-a1c86f0ad38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1241" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503448} {:answer/id #uuid "f31f3176-4cec-53f7-9c8b-c339b62c86e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8159" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503476}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503347} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b47d28-52ea-5db5-8d68-c29eb95437d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c63f903-029e-5df7-9333-d7481c32658b" :question/math-academy-id "q-224997" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(8)$ given below, compute $P(X<9).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0996\\,$ | $\\,0.1912\\,$ | $\\,0.3134\\,$ | $\\,0.4530\\,$ | $\\,0.5925\\,$ | $\\,0.7166\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,8\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 8) \\\\  & =F(8) \\\\  & =0.5925.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b2ab6c64-abb3-5af6-ae48-fa7ca76e80c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503803}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d77a7660-717e-5f04-b7b5-229f67d8428a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2936" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503408}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503408} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dda7cdb-4dec-5905-b4f9-bd936abe49ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f03e36d-2709-59cb-981a-892881905e64" :question/math-academy-id "q-225087" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(3.6)$ given below, compute $P(X\\ge 5).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.5152\\,$ | $\\,0.7064\\,$ | $\\,0.8441\\,$ | $\\,0.9267\\,$ | $\\,0.9692\\,$ | $\\,0.9883\\,$ | $\\,0.9960\\,$ |\n\n$P(X\\ge 5)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nNow, note that \n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=P(X>4).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X>4) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-F(4) \\\\  & =1-0.7064 \\\\  & =0.2936.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cea9281b-2c6b-5557-b332-58513c78c6dd"}] :activity/title "The CDF of the Poisson Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502972}]}
